WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:15.160
Have you ever heard somebody say "I can't watch black and white movies"?

00:00:15.160 --> 00:00:16.260
I have a problem with this

00:00:16.260 --> 00:00:18.760
Not because some of the most important movies are in black and white,

00:00:18.760 --> 00:00:22.040
but because black and white can do just as much if not more than colour.

00:00:22.040 --> 00:00:24.480
The best place to prove this point is film noir,

00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:28.700
a style of filmmaking that continues to influence modern cinematography everywhere

00:00:28.700 --> 00:00:29.980
including Breaking Bad.

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:31.220
But I'm getting ahead of myself.

00:00:31.220 --> 00:00:35.640
I wanna focus on film noir as a style to make the case for black and white filmmaking

00:00:35.640 --> 00:00:37.600
The first question is: What is film noir?

00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:41.520
I actually don't think it requires a concrete definition, because it's something you can feel,

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:43.520
especially in modern parodies.

00:00:44.180 --> 00:00:46.280
"I think I see where this is going."

00:00:46.280 --> 00:00:46.960
"Chicago?"

00:00:46.960 --> 00:00:48.280
"No, the conversation."

00:00:48.280 --> 00:00:49.800
"Am I that predictable?"

00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:52.760
"Only when I can tell what you're gonna do next!"

00:00:55.340 --> 00:00:58.060
Maybe my favorite parody of the style is "Calvin and Hobbes."

00:00:58.060 --> 00:01:01.160
A comic strip can perfectly embody the cinematography of film noir.

00:01:01.160 --> 00:01:05.140
The contrast of white and black, the white trail of cigarette smoke, the long shadows

00:01:05.140 --> 00:01:07.380
and the dramatic, morally ambiguous narrative.

00:01:07.380 --> 00:01:08.540
But let's unpack that.

00:01:08.540 --> 00:01:10.560
To start, let's consider black and white done wrong.

00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:13.960
S&amp;L parodied "Casablanca" recently, and shot in black and white.

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:15.840
"Oh no, Richard, no."

00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:17.440
They are parodying this scene.

00:01:17.440 --> 00:01:19.140
Notice a difference in the look of each shot?

00:01:19.140 --> 00:01:21.820
In the S&amp;L sketch, the contrast is extremely low.

00:01:21.900 --> 00:01:24.380
If I take a sample of the darkest shade in the shot,

00:01:24.380 --> 00:01:26.120
and compare it to the lightest shade in the shot,

00:01:26.120 --> 00:01:28.300
you see there isn't much difference between the two.

00:01:28.300 --> 00:01:30.640
Compare that difference to the difference in "Casablanca".

00:01:30.640 --> 00:01:34.980
The darkest shade on screen is nearly black, while the brightest shade on screen is nearly white.

00:01:34.980 --> 00:01:38.600
This dynamic range of shading makes for a more interesting shot composition.

00:01:38.600 --> 00:01:40.940
And it really showcases how black and white can make

00:01:40.940 --> 00:01:43.080
such an interesting look with higher contrast.

00:01:43.080 --> 00:01:46.040
I know I'm comparing "Casablanca" to a sketch comedy TV show,

00:01:46.040 --> 00:01:48.480
but it does show how black and white can look boring.

00:01:48.480 --> 00:01:50.620
And I think a lot of people see black and white this way.

00:01:50.620 --> 00:01:53.000
Let's see the beauty of black and white in an experiment.

00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:57.260
Let's take a stock photo, in colour, of a staged picture that's got the film noir style.

00:01:57.260 --> 00:01:58.280
Let's try desaturating it,

00:01:58.280 --> 00:02:00.520
upping the contrast and adding some shadow.

00:02:00.520 --> 00:02:03.360
Now look at it. The background isn't distracting from the subject,

00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:05.120
the shadows from the blinds are more pronounced,

00:02:05.120 --> 00:02:07.220
more focus is placed on her facial features,

00:02:07.220 --> 00:02:08.300
like lips and eyebrows.

00:02:08.300 --> 00:02:13.020
The simplicity of black and white means that the eye can observe more key features in the shot.

00:02:13.020 --> 00:02:15.240
Colour's great, but sometimes simple is better.

00:02:15.240 --> 00:02:17.940
In the age of colour, filmmakers going for that noir feel

00:02:17.940 --> 00:02:19.060
have two options.

00:02:19.080 --> 00:02:21.560
The first is mimic noir but in colour.

00:02:21.560 --> 00:02:25.400
Blast the screen with bright, vibrant colours to give the same level of contrast

00:02:25.400 --> 00:02:27.140
and aesthetic as black and white movies.

00:02:27.140 --> 00:02:28.220
Or do the opposite.

00:02:28.220 --> 00:02:31.180
Film in colour but make the colouring so monochromatic

00:02:31.180 --> 00:02:32.720
That it almost looks black and white.

00:02:32.720 --> 00:02:35.140
You can see this in many gritty movies like "Se7en" and "Bound".

00:02:35.140 --> 00:02:37.560
Black and white filmmaking emphasizes contrast,

00:02:37.560 --> 00:02:39.380
so it can be used for visual storytelling.

00:02:39.380 --> 00:02:42.800
The classic blinds shot shows a character that is literally dark and light.

00:02:42.800 --> 00:02:44.480
Lighting is filmed in extremes:

00:02:44.480 --> 00:02:46.080
one side of the character is brightly lit,

00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:47.680
the other side is completely black.

00:02:47.680 --> 00:02:49.500
They've got a good side and a dark side.

00:02:49.500 --> 00:02:51.560
You may not actively acknowledge these details,

00:02:51.560 --> 00:02:53.720
but they subtly remind the viewer, through lighting,

00:02:53.720 --> 00:02:55.100
that the character might have a dark side.

00:02:55.100 --> 00:02:57.360
Or, at least it makes the scene much more threatening.

00:02:57.360 --> 00:02:59.980
Black and white filmmaking gave cinema these techniques.

00:02:59.980 --> 00:03:02.120
We still see them in colour, and they work well,

00:03:02.120 --> 00:03:05.400
but black and white has a much stronger look because of the upped contrast.

00:03:05.400 --> 00:03:08.020
This sequence in "Breaking Bad" screams film noir.

00:03:08.020 --> 00:03:11.220
If I make this black and white, you could even confuse it as an old noir flick.

00:03:11.220 --> 00:03:13.960
I'm not saying "Breaking Bad" should've been filmed in black and white,

00:03:13.960 --> 00:03:17.140
but what I am saying is "Breaking Bad's" gorgeous cinematography

00:03:17.140 --> 00:03:20.380
owes itself to the techniques that shine the strongest in black and white movies.

00:03:20.380 --> 00:03:22.960
Actually, "Breaking Bad" has film noir lighting everywhere.

00:03:22.960 --> 00:03:25.060
The blinds, the good side and the dark side,

00:03:25.160 --> 00:03:27.080
high contrast, and smoking.

00:03:27.080 --> 00:03:29.180
Smoke looks gorgeous in a black and white movie.

00:03:29.180 --> 00:03:32.460
The movie "The Big Combo" shows all the beauty of black and white in this shot.

00:03:32.460 --> 00:03:34.560
Silhouettes, smoke, high contrast.

00:03:34.560 --> 00:03:35.560
It looks fantastic.

00:03:35.560 --> 00:03:38.320
So if you wanna convince somebody that black and white has merit,

00:03:38.320 --> 00:03:42.180
give them a movie that shows off what high contrast and low key lighting can do.

00:03:42.180 --> 00:03:43.180
Show them a film noir.

00:03:43.180 --> 00:03:45.920
Strong lights, strong blacks, strong picture.

00:03:46.260 --> 00:03:49.380
"It's uh, it's got that thing where the blacks are like

00:03:49.840 --> 00:03:52.320
y'know, really really really black"

00:03:52.780 --> 00:03:53.680
Thanks for watching.

